-- ============================================================================
-- Migration 81: reference_entity.lookup_keys — resolve an authority by the
-- lookup key, not by byte-equality (Track PC, decision D2).
--
-- `reference_entity` is matched with `canonical_name = $name` and
-- `$alias IN aliases`: exact, case-sensitive equality, because migrations 41, 55
-- and 57 define no collation. Measured in the D2 experiment on the 18 ministry
-- rows that should link: the K.4 lookup resolved 2; the same lookup
-- case-insensitively resolved 4. And the corpus writes `Koninkrijkrelaties`
-- without the s, a variant `canonicalize_spelling` already knows and the lookup
-- never sees.
--
-- The key is computed in Python by `shared.utils.nl_normalization.
-- normalize_alias_key` (lowercase, whitespace, trailing punctuation, a leading
-- article, curated spelling variants) over the canonical name and every alias,
-- and written by `ReferenceEntityRepository.upsert`. SurrealQL cannot compute
-- it, so this migration only declares the field: rows written before it carry
-- no keys and are found by nothing until the next `refresh()`, which rewrites
-- every row. `reference_entity` held zero rows on `staging` when this was
-- written, so there was nothing to backfill.
-- ============================================================================

DEFINE FIELD IF NOT EXISTS lookup_keys ON reference_entity TYPE array<string> DEFAULT [];
DEFINE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_ref_lookup_keys ON reference_entity FIELDS lookup_keys;
